# Accepted findings for the browser shell image only.
#
# This file is *not* the repository's root .trivyignore and is deliberately not
# merged into it: everything below is a property of a third-party binary this
# image copies in, and none of it should excuse the same CVE in a binary we
# build ourselves. The release workflow passes both files for this image and
# only the root one for the other two — see the `trivyignores` field on the
# matrix in .github/workflows/release.yml.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Go standard library, as linked into helm v3.21.4 (stdlib v1.26.5)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every one of these is fixed in Go 1.26.6, and none of them is fixed in any
# helm release that exists. Trivy reports them as fixable because the *Go*
# fix shipped; --ignore-unfixed therefore does not filter them, and it is the
# toolchain the upstream release was built with rather than anything helm or
# KubeMG chose. Checked at the time of writing: helm v3.21.4 (the newest 3.x)
# and helm v4.2.4 (the newest release of any line) carry byte-identical
# findings, so there is no version to move to — only a build to wait for.
#
# The alternative is compiling helm from source against a current toolchain,
# which this image deliberately does not do. Its whole argument is two tools
# pinned to upstream releases and verified against the checksum those releases
# publish (see the docblock in shell/Dockerfile); a helm binary nobody else
# has is a worse supply chain than a known one with a known toolchain gap.
#
# What the exposure actually is, kind by kind: these are denial-of-service and
# parsing defects reached by feeding hostile input to a *client*. The client
# here is a helm CLI in a per-user pod, driven by an authenticated operator,
# talking to chart repositories that operator chose. It holds no cluster
# credential (see backend/pkg/shell/shell.go), it serves nothing, and nothing
# reaches it unsolicited. The realistic worst case is an operator hanging
# their own shell, which the idle reaper and activeDeadlineSeconds already
# bound. html/template's XSS is rendered into a terminal, not a browser.
#
# Revisit when helm publishes a release built with Go >= 1.26.6, and delete
# this block rather than extending it — a stdlib accept that outlives its
# upstream fix is how an ignore file stops meaning anything.
CVE-2026-33818
CVE-2026-39821
CVE-2026-46600
CVE-2026-56853
CVE-2026-56858
CVE-2026-56859
CVE-2026-56860
CVE-2026-56862

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# oras.land/oras-go/v2 v2.6.1, vendored into helm v3.21.4
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Fixed in oras-go 2.6.2, which no helm release has picked up yet. It is the
# OCI registry client, so it is reached only by `helm pull`/`helm push`
# against an oci:// reference the operator typed. KubeMG's own Helm path never
# uses it: pkg/helm refuses oci:// with its own message and installs from the
# stored catalogue over http/https (see the Helm-as-a-source section of
# ARCHITECTURE.md). Same revisit condition as above.
CVE-2026-50163
